    "body": "Operations at Ontario's Island Gold mine. Credit: Alamos Gold.\nBy: Northern Miner Staff November 25, 2026\nA proposed Canadian tax break could leave new gold mines with about 30% more free cash flow in their first years of production, according to a BMO Capital Markets model. Larger miners with existing Canadian operations could see a more modest gain of about 3% in first-year operating cash flow, tapering after that, BMO’s mining analysts said late Tuesday in a report. Alamos Gold (TSX, NYSE: AGI) and Wesdome Gold Mines (TSX: WDO; US-OTC: WDOFF) are among the producers they expect to benefit most. Getting the deductions sooner “could help some marginal projects or brownfield expansions meet companies’ investment thresholds,” the Mining Association of Canada said on Sept. 15 when Ottawa announced the proposal. Ottawa’s proposed Productivity Mega Deduction would allow miners to deduct the full cost of qualifying Canadian development expenses incurred on or after Sept. 15, against taxable income in the year the expense was incurred. Eligible equipment acquired from that date could also be fully written off once it is available for use, the Finance Department said. Many capital deductions are currently spread over several years. The proposal would make immediate expensing permanent. It accelerates tax deductions rather than reimbursing companies for their spending.\nValuable overlap\nFor an established producer, deductions still owing on earlier investments would overlap with the full writeoff on new spending. That overlap is most valuable in the first year and fades as the old deductions are exhausted. A company already using accumulated losses to shelter its income would see less immediate benefit, BMO said. Tax remittance schedules would also affect when cash savings appear. Alamos spent $131.7 million (US$92.1 million) in growth capital at its Island Gold district in Ontario in the third quarter of 2026, much of it on the shaft, underground development and the Magino mill expansion. Alamos expects to commission the shaft in mid-2027, about one quarter later than previously planned, and finish expanding the mill in the first quarter of 2028, the company said in October . The tax proposal’s Sept. 15 eligibility date falls partway through that reporting period. The tax change would add about 7% to the net asset value of BMO’s model gold project, compared with about 2% for larger producers with Canadian operations, the bank said. The hypothetical developer’s internal rate of return would rise to 21% from 20%. BMO’s estimates assume provinces will follow Ottawa’s lead.\n“This may seem minor, but the uplift in the near-term cash flow is meaningful,” the BMO analysts wrote.\nM&A boost\nExplorers and developers poised to benefit from larger tax deductions once their projects enter production include Snowline Gold (TSX: SGD; US-OTC: SNWGF), Troilus Mining (TSX: TLG; US-OTC: CHXMF) and Osisko Metals (TSX: OM; US-OTC: OMZNF), BMO said. However, limited taxable income early in a mine’s life could delay the cash savings for these companies, the bank said. Ottawa’s proposed mega-deduction could also encourage acquisitions by fully taxable producers, which could use eligible deductions from project spending to offset income from existing Canadian operations while adding future production, BMO said. Federal legislation is still pending. Ottawa reaffirmed its intention to proceed with the measure in the government’s most recent budget , which finance minister François-Philippe Champagne introduced Nov. 5.\nPrint\nTAG: financing gold mining gold projects Taxation\nCOMMODITY: Copper Gold graphite Iron Ore molybdenum Rare Earths Silver Specialty Minerals Uranium\nREGION: Canada\nCOMPANY: Alamos Gold Osisko Metals Snowline Gold Troilus Mining Wesdome Gold Mines\nRepublish this article",
